who is the riddler is he edward nigma or  edward nashton a wisecracking eagle  maniac or a psychopathic killer and how  does paul dano's version of the  character compare to the rest keep  watching for the answers to all these  riddles and more  even casual batman fans know the  riddler's real name edward nigma it's a  pretty obvious pun enigma sounds like  the word enigma referencing the  mysteries and puzzles that the riddler  loves to incorporate into his crimes  some viewers may be surprised in that he  has a slightly different alias in the  batman going instead by the less  familiar edward nashton that name wasn't  invented for the movie though in dc  comics it's the riddler's real name at  least it was for 25 years between 1986  and 2011 the period known in dc  continuity as new earth nashton was  riddler's canonical name though he did  change it to enigma as part of his  criminal ambitions after the events of  flashpoint however new earth gave way to  the new 52 in prime earth and the  riddler's real name reverted back to  edward nigma but with a y this time  live-action iterations of the character  in the past have used the surname migma  with both spellings including those  found in batman forever and the tv  series gotham while recognizable it's  fair to say that this name is kinda  campy too such an on the nose reference  to the character's obsessions doesn't  really fit with the tone or aesthetics  of the batman as a result matt reeves  movie has given a live-action iteration  of the character the name edward nashton  for the very first time  the most famous orphan in gotham city is  undoubtedly bruce wayne a child of  wealthy sociolites whose tragic deaths  were no secret to the population at  large in the batman's version of the  tale bruce became something of a recluse  following the deaths of his parents at  least that's the way the public saw it  in truth he was biting his time and  completing his training to emerge as the  dark knight still bruce rarely shows his  face in public the batman's riddler has  a bone to pick with bruce in particular  mostly because of the sins of his father  thomas wayne who purportedly associated  with the mob however in a notable change  from most other versions of the  character this riddler is jealous of  bruce the famous orphan the one who  didn't have to live in squalor in a  decrepit wayne orphanage that was the  harsh childhood the riddler endured  never receiving the proper care or  attention that displaced children  deserve instead he grew to resent the  city and the people that took from him  and others like him little did the  corrupt gotham leadership realize that  their greed would give birth to their  future demise  the riddler is typically portrayed as an  egomaniac with a morbid funny bone who  always fancies himself the smartest man  in the room being of superior intellect  he can't help but laugh and mock those  beneath him which is just about everyone  both the frank gorshan and jim carrey  versions of the character find humor in  their own hijinks they're outlandish and  cartoony villains who crack jokes make  puns and belittle their opposition with  sarcastic wit  caffeine will kill you  the batman on the other hand presents a  riddler absolutely devoid of any such  humor displaying a kind of subtle rage  instead like many batman villains he's a  dark reflection of the dark knight they  both exact vengeance on the wicked they  both wear masks and they both warn  criminals to fear them their intellects  are comparable too but this riddler  isn't nearly as fixated on his own  superiority where previous incarnations  were mischievous and colorful this  riddler is dark and terrifying a  psychopathic stalker who horrifically  bludgeons his victims in the shadows of  their own homes he still deals in  puzzles short but paul dano's  performance creates a striking portrait  of an unhinged man unleashing his own  brand of righteous fury upon gotham city  the riddler of the batman would fit  comfortably in a slasher movie and  unlike his predecessors he has precious  little to laugh about  the riddler's plots are pretty much  always foiled by batman sure he's killed  many people over the years and has  enacted plenty of destructive schemes  but it's rare for any villain to  actually succeed in wreaking real  devastation on gothel in the third act  of the batman however the riddler  actually succeeds in his grand plan  unleashing a flood to cleanse the city  of its filth batman is still something  of an amateur at this point and this is  very much a movie in which batman makes  mistakes including failing to deduce the  riddler's final plan in time the film's  true resolution is batman's  acknowledgement that he must be more for  the people of gotham than a vigilante  pounding baddies into the pavement every  night instead he must be a symbol of  hope when batman is seen providing aid  and relief to victims of the flooding on  the news in arkham the riddler is  angered the last thing he wanted to do  was bring a sense of unity to the people  of gotham and bolster a new champion for  the people still ridler did at least win  the battle even if he won't win the war  in the colorful pages of dc comics the  riddler is often as pompous as they come  he would never happily share the  spotlight as he simply craves it too  much the riddler is destined to be a  one-man show and he'll live out the rest  of his days trying to one-up and defeat  batman if it's the last thing he does  despite the riddler's comic book  predilection for hoarding all the  notoriety paul dano's take on the  character is noticeably different while  the riddler obviously prides himself on  his capability to toy with law  enforcement he ultimately has one  objective in mind justice in the face of  corruption as such the riddler finds a  sense of kinship with the dark knight  seemingly a crusader for justice just  like him later in the film the batman  reveals that the riddler's messages for  the batman weren't a taunting game they  were an invitation to join forces for  most of the film the riddler believes  the batman is actually on his side  simply because their goals seem to align  they'll remember me now  they'll remember both of us  it's only when the riddler is locked up  that he realizes the batman was never  actually aiding his cause  the riddler's aggression is typically  levied against the weak and powerful  alike his primary objective is to  elevate himself as a notoriously brainy  criminal and to exert dominion overall  especially batman that's simply the  nature of the game for an extreme  narcissist like the riddler and the  previous live-action versions of the  character all fit the mold of a  power-hungry megalomaniac bent on  destroying batman it's extremely rare  then to see the riddler portrayed as an  anti-hero of sorts there are a couple of  exceptions the 2013 video game batman  arkham origins includes an early career  edward nigma who has hidden extortion  data all throughout the city in an  attempt to highlight gotham's deep rock  also like in the batman the version of  the character from batman earth 1 is  also a serial killer waging war on the  corrupt these are the only instances in  recent memory in which the riddler  flirts with anti-hero status however  until the batman which clearly counted  earth 1 among its inspirations the  riddler's war is seemingly a righteous  one in his own eyes he wants nothing  more than to see the evil and greedy  elite of gotham suffer and pay for their  injustices it's a riddler we haven't  seen before in live action and it adds a  whole other layer to this deeply  tortured character  the riddler is probably kidding himself  when he says he's not mentally unstable  like the rest of batman's rivals after  all a certain degree of obsession must  be involved for him to commit to a  never-ending conflict with a man dressed  like a bat but killing isn't a highlight  of the riddler's career it's merely a  means to an end typically the  puzzle-spinning crook finds joy in  outwitting others especially a prominent  and intelligent figure like the batman  daniel's riddler certainly has a few  screws loose but that's the result of a  lifetime of neglect living as an outcast  in a cruel city like gotham in the  batman the riddler seems to be the exact  opposite of his comic book counterpart  the riddles are a means to an end not  the end themselves this riddler isn't  seeking to outsmart anyone he derives  joy not from a feeling of superiority  but from the satisfaction of knowing  he's about to end someone who's done the  city dirty he also savors his kills the  introductory scene in which the riddler  stalks the mayor in his own home is  chilling but he doesn't kill his target  immediately even though he has the  chance the mayor's family is out  trick-or-treating and no one else is  around yet the riddler remains in the  shadows apparently enjoying the moment  as much as possible it's a long long way  from this guy  adios amigos  see you in court  the batman paints a bleak and quite  original picture of edward nashton's  history nashton's troubles began growing  up in wayne manor repurposed to be an  orphanage and seeing both the building  and the lives of his fellow orphans fall  into disrepair when the funding dried up  thanks to gotham's greed the orphans  were forced to live in squalor  fortunately edward developed his  signature coping mechanism early on even  as a child he fixated on solving puzzles  the hobby helped him to bide his time  and turn his focus away from his tragic  life nashton's pension for enigmas  served him later in life when he became  a forensic accountant a career choice  that led directly to him taking up arms  against the corrupt the skills he gained  enabled him to piece together the theft  of thomas wayne's renewal fund which was  ripe for the picking after wayne's  untimely death that was the moment  nashton realized he owed his dark past  to gotham's shady elite and vowed to  bring them down  every villain needs henchmen there's no  way men like the riddler could contend  with batman physically on their own as  the dark knight is far too great of a  physical force to be reckoned with loyal  guns are essential to making a splash in  gotham city and most of the time  villains already have them at hand when  the story begins  we're usually left to infer that the  henchmen are either well paid terrified  or both and while that's probably a  reasonable assumption the batman does  things a little differently the batman  finds the riddler living in the modern  age of social media attracting hundreds  of followers throughout gotham via  online ramblings about his crusade  thanks for all the comments and a  special thanks to everyone for the tips  on detonators  essentially the batman's riddler  radicalizes and recruits followers for  his war against gotham's elite those  like-minded individuals who have also  been victims of corruption show up in  mass to help assassinate the newly  elected mayor bella real and terrorize  the people of the city it's an  impressively modern twist on villainy in  an age of widespread online interaction  the riddler often has a distinct design  that's instantly recognizable to most  comic book fans outfitted with  sunglasses or a black or green domino  mask he always comes off as something of  a fashionista donning a green bowler hat  and a green suit adorned in question  marks there have been several variations  on that theme over the years including  jim carrey's eccentric showstopper in  1995's batman forever but the riddler's  appearance has typically been as  distinct as it is familiar in contrast  the batman presents a villain who seeks  to conceal his identity for obvious  reasons wearing little more than a mask  and practical clothing this new attire  makes complete sense in the world of the  batman where getting revenge means  getting your clothes dirty along with  your hands daniel's riddler has neither  time nor use for flashy spectacle his  costume design is simple yet iconic  conveying everything the people of  gotham need to know about the man  wreaking havoc on their city he's  enigmatic he's practical and he means  business check out one of our newest  videos right here plus even more looper  videos about your favorite movies are  coming soon subscribe to our youtube  channel and hit the bell so you don't  miss a single one  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]
 

